From bf0a34777329ba109556588631d6a244c8f51cd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Zaugg <jzaugg@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:15:55 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Merkle: name library ancestors that are nested or take type
 arguments

LibraryAncestors names a class's library ancestors to match them against
the classes of a changed jar, and to find the jars whose stamps go into
its hashes. It named them with Discovery.simpleName, which gives no name
for a parent with type arguments, `extends L[Int]`, nor for a class
nested in an object, `extends O.L`: the bridge renders that as a
projection on the object's type, Projection(Projection(..., O), L). So
neither counted as a library ancestor. When the jar changed, D extends
L recompiled (it refers to the jar) but no descendant did, and a
descendant whose override no longer conformed compiled against the old
signature: `E extends D { def m = 1 }` with L.m now a String. Name
hashing materialises m in D's API and recompiles E.

Name ancestors with MerkleHashes.typeName, which drops type arguments,
and which now follows a projection on a projection. MerkleHashes'
linearization, from which composition takes a class's ancestors, names
them the same way, so an ancestor nested in an object of another
subproject is composed too.

merkle-libjar-override-nested and merkle-libjar-override-parameterized
are merkle-libjar-override with the library ancestor nested in an object
and taking a type argument; both fail without this change.
merkle-x-member-type-nested is merkle-x-member-type with the upstream
ancestor nested in an object, which passes either way: the client
depends on the ancestor directly.
